---
id: org.dxos.spec.app
name: DSL.App
version: 1.0
extends:
  - org.dxos.spec.core
---

The app dialect: vocabulary for describing a Composer application's
**navigation and presentation structure** — app-graph nodes, deck level
chains, planks, companions, surface contributions, and menus. It complements
the standard dialect (`type`, `op`, `component`, `service`, `feat`, `test`),
which describes data and behavior; this dialect describes *where things
appear and how the user reaches them*.

Consumers list the terms they use in their Extensions table (each resolves to
a URI defined here), or extend this document wholesale via frontmatter
`extends`. See `packages/plugins/plugin-inbox/PLUGIN.mdl` for a full
real-world usage.

## Model

The vocabulary mirrors the runtime exactly:

- The **app-graph** is a reactive graph of nodes contributed by plugins.
  A `node` block declares a node (or family of nodes) — its subject, its
  children (e.g. folders), and the actions that hang off it.
- The **deck** renders planks; a plank is *just a graph-node id* whose content
  resolves through an `article` Surface dispatch. A `deck` block declares a
  **level chain** (`Mailbox > Message > Attachment`): opening at a level
  reuses that level's plank and closes levels below.
- A **companion** is a child node (`~variant` linked segment) rendered as a
  plank attached to its context plank.
- A **surface** contribution maps `(role, subject type)` to a component — the
  single dispatch mechanism by which anything renders anywhere.
- A **menu** declares toolbar/context-menu content as references to
  operations (`op:` blocks) and graph-node actions — never inline behavior.

### Reference forms

Cross-references follow the core rules (`[id]`, `op:Name`, `component:Name`)
with these additional prefixes: `node:Name`, `surface:Name`, `type:Name`.
Subjects reference ECHO types declared in `type` blocks (or external types
listed in prose).

## Extensions

| Term        | URI                           |
| ----------- | ----------------------------- |
| `node`      | `org.dxos.mdl.node@1.0`      |
| `deck`      | `org.dxos.mdl.deck@1.0`      |
| `plank`     | `org.dxos.mdl.plank@1.0`     |
| `companion` | `org.dxos.mdl.companion@1.0` |
| `surface`   | `org.dxos.mdl.surface@1.0`   |
| `menu`      | `org.dxos.mdl.menu@1.0`      |

## Definitions

```mdl
ext node
  uri: org.dxos.mdl.node@1.0
  desc: |
    An app-graph node contribution: a node (or per-object family of nodes)
    the plugin adds to the graph. `source` names the parent — a well-known
    node id (e.g. the space root) or a type whose instances the connector
    expands. Children declared inline become child nodes (e.g. folders).
    Actions reference `op:` blocks and surface as menu/toolbar items wherever
    the node appears.
  fields:
    desc?: Prose
    source?: NodeRef | TypeRef   # parent node or the type this connector expands
    subject?: TypeRef | Literal  # the node's data (an ECHO object or a constant)
    properties?: FieldMap        # label, icon, disposition, position, ...
    children?: NodeList          # inline child node declarations (folders etc.)
    actions?: OpRefList          # operations contributed to this node
    article?: SurfaceRef | ComponentRef  # what an article surface renders for it
  nesting: self
```

```mdl
ext deck
  uri: org.dxos.mdl.deck@1.0
  desc: |
    A declared level chain: how reading down a hierarchy maps onto planks.
    Each level names the plank that opens at that depth; opening at a level
    reuses that level's plank and closes deeper ones. Modifier gestures
    (meta/ctrl) opt out and open a free plank instead.
  fields:
    desc?: Prose
    chain: PlankRefList          # ordered, e.g. Mailbox > Message > Attachment
```

```mdl
ext plank
  uri: org.dxos.mdl.plank@1.0
  desc: |
    One deck plank kind: which subject it presents and through which article.
    `parent` places it in a level chain (equivalently declared via `deck`);
    `companions` lists the companion variants available on it.
  fields:
    desc?: Prose
    subject: TypeRef             # the graph node data this plank presents
    article: SurfaceRef | ComponentRef
    parent?: PlankRef            # the level above in the chain
    companions?: CompanionRefList
```

```mdl
ext companion
  uri: org.dxos.mdl.companion@1.0
  desc: |
    A plank companion: a child node (`~variant` linked segment) rendered as a
    plank attached to its context plank, sharing its attention context.
  fields:
    desc?: Prose
    of: TypeRef | NodeRef        # the context plank's subject
    variant: Identifier          # the `~variant` segment / tab id
    component?: ComponentRef | SurfaceRef
    properties?: FieldMap        # label, icon, position, ...
```

```mdl
ext surface
  uri: org.dxos.mdl.surface@1.0
  desc: |
    A Surface contribution: maps a role token plus a subject-type filter to a
    component. This is the app's single presentation dispatch — articles,
    sections, cards, dialogs, and companions all resolve through it.

    Resolution is not most-specific-wins: every contribution whose role and
    filter match is a candidate, and `position` orders them (`first` /
    `last` are the sentinels; equal positions keep registration order). A
    role-only contribution therefore competes with a subject-specific one,
    and the subject-specific one must claim an earlier position to win.
  fields:
    desc?: Prose
    role: Identifier             # role token (article | section | card | dialog | ...)
    subject?: TypeRef            # type filter; omitted = role-only match
    component: ComponentRef
    variant?: Identifier
    position?: number | first | last  # tie-break among all matching contributions
```

```mdl
ext menu
  uri: org.dxos.mdl.menu@1.0
  desc: |
    Toolbar / context-menu content for a node or type: ordered references to
    operations and action groups. Menus carry no behavior of their own —
    every item resolves to an `op:` (directly or through a graph-node
    action).
  fields:
    desc?: Prose
    of: TypeRef | NodeRef        # what the menu attaches to
    disposition?: toolbar | context | list-item
    items: OpRefList             # ordered; groups as nested lists
```

## Example

A mailbox with system folders, a two-level reading chain, and a companion:

```mdl
node MailboxNode
  source: type:Mailbox
  subject: type:Mailbox
  properties:
    icon: ph--tray--regular
  children:
    - Inbox    # folder: system-tag filtered view of the mailbox
    - Sent
    - Drafts
  actions: [op:SyncMailbox, op:DraftEmail]
```

```mdl
plank MailboxPlank
  subject: type:Mailbox
  article: component:MailboxArticle
```

```mdl
plank MessagePlank
  subject: type:Message
  article: component:MessageArticle
  parent: MailboxPlank
```

```mdl
deck MailboxDeck
  desc: Reading down the mailbox reuses one plank per level (two levels shown; the attachment level is elided).
  chain: MailboxPlank > MessagePlank
```

```mdl
companion RelatedContacts
  of: type:Message
  variant: contacts
  component: component:RelatedContacts
```
